\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether the atlas results (69.6--85.4\%) reflect genuine statistical regularities or training artifacts, evaluating four axes: classifier choice (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}), cross-model transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossmodel}), cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}), and end-to-end learning (\S\ref{sec:gen:e2e}).

\subsection{Baseline Comparison}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} evaluates three classifier families---bagging (RF), gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM)---on the same 19 features across three atlas cells spanning the full accuracy range.
Per-cell spread is $\leq$0.4pp, establishing a feature-determined ceiling independent of the learning algorithm.
On Pythia nucleus, CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match the within-cell RF of Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} within 0.1\,pp (bootstrap $p>0.05$, Bonferroni-corrected); all feature-based methods significantly outperform CE-MLP (74.9\%, $p<0.001$) and logistic regression (75.0\%).
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); a zero-shot LLM (DeepSeek V3.2) reaches only 36.2\% (95\% CI $[32.0, 40.4]$\%, 500 samples).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Classifier convergence: 4-class accuracy (\%) across three atlas cells. RF (bagging), XGBoost and LightGBM (gradient boosting) operate on the same 19 features; $\Delta_{\max}$: largest per-cell spread. Chance = 25\%. RF values differ from Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} by $\leq$0.7pp due to matched-fold evaluation.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Cell & RF & XGB & LGBM & $\Delta_{\max}$ \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc. & 78.8 & 79.0 & 78.9 & 0.2 \\
GPT-2~XL, $\tau$ & 85.8 & 86.2 & 86.2 & 0.4 \\
Pythia, top-$k$ & 68.9 & 68.9 & 68.7 & 0.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Convergence across five structurally different classifiers---spanning bagging, gradient boosting, ordinal regression, and ensemble detection---confirms the signal resides in the features, not the learning algorithm.
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified; errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread across d1--d3 under transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.4\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 bal.\ acc: Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.2\% (mean 55.4\%).}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline while d2 drops to 26.8\% (Pythia holdout).
Excluding d0, d1--d3 balanced accuracy averages 55.4\% (chance = 33\%).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

We train on two decoding strategies and test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7pp below cross-model transfer (66.4\%); excluding d0, d1--d3 accuracy drops to 45.9\% (chance = 33\%).
d2 recall varies sharply: nucleus holdouts retain 30--54\%, top-$k$ holdouts fall to 8--19\%, and temperature holdouts span 3.7--39.5\% (median 8.9\%).
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature inverts the d1/d2 boundary (d1v2 AUC $<$ 0.5); per-strategy z-score normalization partially recovers it but leaves a residual gap (Limitations).

\subsection{End-to-End Learning}
\label{sec:gen:e2e}

DeBERTa-v3-base \cite{he2021_deberta_v3} and RoBERTa-base \cite{liu2019_roberta} finetuned end-to-end achieve 58.3\% ($\pm$4.7\%) and 59.5\% ($\pm$3.2\%) respectively---lagging the RF baseline (78.2\%) by 19.9pp and 18.7pp.
The 1.2pp inter-backbone gap rules out architecture bias; Table~\ref{tab:e2e_detail} shows both backbones share the same failure mode---high d0 F1 ($>$0.94) but collapsed d1/d2 performance (F1 $\leq$ 0.42)---while RF achieves 0.596 d2 recall, a $>$2$\times$ advantage at the hardest depth class.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Per-class performance for end-to-end PLM classifiers vs.\ RF baseline (Pythia-1.4B nucleus). PLM columns report F1; RF d2 column reports recall (the class with the largest PLM--RF gap). Both PLM backbones share the same failure pattern: collapsed d1/d2 scores despite adequate d0/d3.}
\label{tab:e2e_detail}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Method & Acc (\%) & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
\midrule
DeBERTa-v3 & 58.3\,$\pm$\,4.7 & .947 & .378 & .247 & .656 \\
RoBERTa-base & 59.5\,$\pm$\,3.2 & .958 & .416 & .261 & .647 \\
\midrule
RF (19 feat.) & 78.2 & --- & --- & .596$^\dagger$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{6}{@{}l}{\footnotesize $^\dagger$Recall; shown for the depth class with the largest PLM--RF gap.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Feature space divergence.}
SHAP TreeExplainer analysis confirms that perplexity and surprisal features account for 88.8\% of RF's total contribution.
Three independent importance measures---SHAP, Gini, and permutation---show strong concordance (Kendall's $W{=}0.90$, $p{<}0.005$; pairwise Spearman $\rho$: SHAP--Gini $0.93$, SHAP--permutation $0.84$; Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}), with \texttt{p90\_ppl} appearing in the SHAP top-2 in 9/9 cells and \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 8/9.
Linear probing of PLM [CLS] representations yields negative $R^2$ for both \texttt{var\_surprisal} and \texttt{p90\_ppl} (Appendix~\ref{app:feature_overlap}), indicating that pretrained representations lack the linear structure needed to recover perplexity tail statistics---a systematic representational gap rather than a backbone-specific artifact.

DCOL \cite{cloc2025} reached 64.5\% (Appendix~\ref{app:dcol_iterations}); all end-to-end approaches (58--65\%) underperform feature-based methods, suggesting depth estimation benefits from targeted statistical features that capture distributional tail properties absent from PLM representations.
This comparison is bounded by backbone scale ($\leq$125M parameters); whether larger PLMs (e.g., 7B+) can close the gap via richer internal representations remains an open question.

\paragraph{Ecological validity.}
On WikiText-103 (546 editorial passages), all three model-specific RF classifiers achieve $\geq$98.5\% d0 accuracy despite $+$621\% domain shift in mean perplexity---partly an easy case, as WikiText's higher perplexity places it farther from d1--d3 in feature space.
On noisier web-crawl data (5{,}000 C4 passages), FPR ranges from 4.7\% (OLMo) to 22.4\% (GPT-2~XL), concentrated in template/SEO content with high n-gram repetition (Table~\ref{tab:calibration}); deployment on heterogeneous web data requires model-specific calibration.

\section{The Depth Degradation Atlas}
\label{sec:atlas}

We present the Depth Degradation Atlas: a systematic empirical map of how statistical fingerprints evolve across recursive generation depths, models, and decoding strategies.
The atlas spans $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells evaluated at depths $d \in \{0, 1, 2, 3\}$, with depth extensions to $d{=}5$ for cross-model EDD validation (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{In-Distribution Performance}
\label{sec:atlas:id}

Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} reports four-class Random Forest accuracy (5-fold CV, 200 trees) across all nine cells.
Accuracy ranges from 69.6\% (Pythia, top-$k$) to 85.4\% (GPT-2~XL, temperature), all exceeding the 25\% chance baseline by a wide margin.
Two axis effects emerge.
GPT-2~XL achieves the highest accuracy under every strategy (76.6--85.4\%), followed by OLMo (74.2--82.0\%) and Pythia (69.6--78.2\%).
Top-$k$ sampling yields the lowest accuracy in every model.
The mean within-model strategy gap is 8.4~pp, comparable to the 7.1~pp mean within-strategy model gap---decoding strategy and model family contribute roughly equally to performance variance.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Four-class RF accuracy (\%) across the $3 \times 3$ atlas, with d1--d3 three-class accuracy in parentheses (chance = 25\% / 33\%). Depth-0 recall exceeds 98\% in all cells.}
\label{tab:main_matrix}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
& Nucleus & Temp.\ $\tau{=}0.9$ & Top-$k$ \\
\midrule
Pythia-1.4B  & 78.2\,(71.2) & 76.3\,(70.6) & 69.6\,(58.8) \\
OLMo-1B     & 78.8\,(75.0) & 82.0\,(76.3) & 74.2\,(66.1) \\
GPT-2~XL    & 83.3\,(79.0) & 85.4\,(82.6) & 76.6\,(69.5) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Per-class recall distribution.}
Depth-0 recall exceeds 98\% in all 9 cells, confirming that human-written text is trivially separable from any generation depth.
Among generated depths, d2 consistently yields the lowest recall (46--73\% across cells), followed by d3 (58--82\%) and d1 (72--91\%).
For example, Pythia-1.4B nucleus achieves per-class recall of d0$\,{=}\,$99\%, d1$\,{=}\,$82\%, d2$\,{=}\,$59\%, d3$\,{=}\,$72\%.
The strongest d2 recall is obtained by GPT-2~XL nucleus (73\%).
The d2 trough reflects its position at the center of the depth gradient: d2 features overlap with both the d1 distribution above and d3 below, making it the most confusable class---consistent with the d2-vs-d3 pair being the EDD bottleneck (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).
Full per-class recall for all cells appears in Appendix~\ref{app:per_class}.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Feature Consistency Across the Grid}
\label{sec:atlas:features}

Despite the 15.8~pp accuracy spread across cells, a small feature family dominates in every setting.
The 90th-percentile perplexity (\texttt{p90\_ppl}) ranks top-2 by Gini importance in all 9 cells; surprisal variance (\texttt{var\_surprisal}) in 8/9 under SHAP (7/9 under Gini; Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}).
Table~\ref{tab:feature_importance} shows the full per-cell ranking.

Cross-model feature-rank Spearman correlation averages $\bar{\rho} = 0.868$ (range $[0.619, 0.954]$): models agree on which features carry depth information even when they differ in absolute accuracy.
Among strategies, top-$k$ yields the highest cross-model rank correlation ($\rho = 0.811$)---the deterministic vocabulary truncation imposes a more uniform feature landscape across architectures, at the cost of weaker overall signal (\S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}).

Percentile-based features outperform their moment-based counterparts.
Token-level perplexity distributions are heavy-tailed: individual outlier passages can dominate the arithmetic mean of their depth class, whereas \texttt{p90\_ppl} is robust to such extremes while still capturing the tail erosion that signals generation depth.

Feature ablation confirms this concentration: the top-2 features (\texttt{p90\_ppl} + \texttt{var\_surprisal}) alone reach 91.1\% of full accuracy (71.5\%), and the top-5 features achieve 97.6\% (76.5\% vs.\ 78.4\%), with perplexity statistics alone reaching 76.6\% (vs.\ surprisal 73.2\%, other categories ${\leq}$47\%).
Variance inflation analysis reveals VIF${>}$10 for 14--16 of 19 features, placing the effective independent dimensionality at ${\approx}$4--5.
Forward selection independently confirms the dominant pair: \texttt{p90\_ppl} is selected first in 6/9 cells, \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 3/9.

The feature hierarchy also shifts with depth.
At shallow boundaries (d1 vs.\ d2), \texttt{var\_surprisal} dominates importance; at deeper boundaries (d3 vs.\ d4, d4 vs.\ d5), tail perplexity statistics (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{kurtosis\_ppl}) take over.
This transition aligns with model-collapse dynamics~\cite{shumailov2024_nature}: early recursion narrows the surprisal distribution (captured by its variance), while later steps erode the perplexity tail (captured by upper quantiles and kurtosis).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Top-5 features by Gini importance. \textbf{Bold}: top-2. \texttt{p90\_ppl} top-2 in 9/9; \texttt{var\_surprisal} 8/9 under SHAP (7/9 under Gini).}
\label{tab:feature_importance}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l l@{}}
\toprule
Cell & Top-5 features (Gini importance, ranked) \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc.       & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.17)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.15)}, m\_ppl\,(.11), var\_ppl\,(.08), p75\,(.07) \\
Pythia, $\tau$     & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.17)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.14)}, m\_ppl\,(.11), p75\,(.07), m\_surp\,(.07) \\
Pythia, top-$k$    & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.16)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.12)}, m\_ppl\,(.11), var\_ppl\,(.07), m\_surp\,(.06) \\
GPT-2\,XL, nuc.    & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.21)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.21)}, p75\,(.10), m\_surp\,(.07), m\_ppl\,(.06) \\
GPT-2\,XL, $\tau$  & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.25)}, \textbf{p75\,(.15)}, m\_surp\,(.13), var\_surp\,(.12), p50\,(.05) \\
GPT-2\,XL, top-$k$ & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.24)}, \textbf{p75\,(.12)}, var\_surp\,(.10), m\_surp\,(.09), ttr\,(.04) \\
OLMo, nuc.         & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.18)}, \textbf{var\_surp\,(.15)}, p75\,(.11), m\_surp\,(.07), ttr\,(.06) \\
OLMo, $\tau$       & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.21)}, \textbf{var\_surp\,(.16)}, p75\,(.12), m\_surp\,(.09), ttr\,(.05) \\
OLMo, top-$k$      & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.20)}, \textbf{var\_surp\,(.17)}, p75\,(.10), m\_surp\,(.07), m\_ppl\,(.05) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_atlas_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Atlas feature importance heatmap. Rows: 19 features grouped by category (\S\ref{sec:method:features}); columns: 9 model--strategy cells. Color intensity encodes RF Gini importance. The perplexity-tail and surprisal-variance blocks maintain high importance across all cells.}
\label{fig:atlas_heatmap}
\end{figure}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Monotonicity and Inter-Depth Distances}
\label{sec:atlas:monotonicity}

Depth features exhibit strong monotonic trends: perplexity tail statistics (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{mean\_surprisal}) decrease with depth (Spearman $\rho = -1.0$ across cell-level medians) while repetition features increase, reflecting the complementary narrowing and self-repetition of distribution collapse.
Inter-depth distances are non-uniform: d0-vs-d1 AUC exceeds 0.99 in all 9 cells, whereas d2-vs-d3 ranges from 0.69 to 0.89---separating adjacent deep generations requires the tail statistics identified in \S\ref{sec:atlas:features}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_feature_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{Feature trajectories across depths $d{=}0$ to $d{=}3$, stratified by model (columns) and strategy (line style). Top row: \texttt{p90\_ppl} (monotonically decreasing). Middle: \texttt{var\_surprisal} (decreasing). Bottom: \texttt{rep\_3gram} (increasing). Shaded bands: interquartile range. The $d{=}0 \to d{=}1$ gap exceeds all subsequent inter-depth gaps.}
\label{fig:feature_trajectory}
\end{figure}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{EDD Analysis}
\label{sec:atlas:edd}

We extend the benchmark to depth $d{=}5$ for all three models under nucleus sampling.
Each model's depth-3 LoRA checkpoint is used to finetune and generate depth-4 data, and the process repeats for depth~5; feature extraction uses the same reference model (GPT-2 base, 124M).
We train dedicated binary RF classifiers (500 trees) for each adjacent pair, as defined in Eq.~\ref{eq:edd}.

\paragraph{AUC decay profile.}
Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} plots the binary pairwise AUC decay curves.
For Pythia-1.4B (nucleus), AUC decays monotonically: d0-vs-d1 $= 0.999$, d1-vs-d2 $= 0.925$, d2-vs-d3 $= 0.823$, d3-vs-d4 $= 0.784$, d4-vs-d5 $= 0.683$.
The steepest drop occurs between the d1-vs-d2 and d2-vs-d3 pairs; the curve then flattens as the system approaches an indistinguishable regime gradually rather than crossing a sharp boundary.

\paragraph{Cross-model convergence at depth 4--5.}
The d3-vs-d4 AUC consistently clusters near the $T{=}0.75$ threshold across all three models (Pythia $0.784$, OLMo $0.765$, GPT-2~XL $0.750$), while d4-vs-d5 uniformly falls below $0.70$ ($0.681$--$0.684$).
The directional pattern is consistent, but statistical strength is stratified: bootstrap testing ($10{,}000$ resamples, $H_0{:}\, \mathrm{AUC} \leq 0.75$) yields $p = 0.001$ for Pythia (significant), $p = 0.091$ for OLMo (marginal), and $p = 0.505$ for GPT-2~XL (non-significant).
All three models cross the $T{=}0.75$ threshold within the d3--d4 region, establishing a consistent depth-4 decay boundary with graded confidence rather than uniform statistical significance.
The d4-vs-d5 saturation is unambiguous across models, supporting the interpretation of d3--d4 as a \emph{transition zone} from discriminable to indistinguishable regime.

\paragraph{EDD threshold sensitivity.}
Table~\ref{tab:edd_sensitivity} reports $\mathrm{EDD}_T$ across the primary 9-cell benchmark (depths 0--3) at four threshold values.
At $T{=}0.65$, all 9 cells achieve $\mathrm{EDD} = 3$; at $T{=}0.75$, 7 of 9 cells maintain $\mathrm{EDD} \geq 3$; at $T{=}0.80$, the count drops to 5 of 9.
The cells losing $\mathrm{EDD}{=}3$ first are consistently the top-$k$ configurations (Pythia top-$k$ at $T{=}0.70$, then OLMo top-$k$ at $T{=}0.75$), where the d2-vs-d3 pair is the consistent bottleneck across all 9 cells.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Number of atlas cells achieving $\mathrm{EDD} \geq 3$ at each threshold $T$, across 9 model--strategy cells (depths 0--3). The d2-vs-d3 boundary is the bottleneck; top-$k$ cells drop first.}
\label{tab:edd_sensitivity}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
& $T{=}0.65$ & $T{=}0.70$ & $T{=}0.75$ & $T{=}0.80$ \\
\midrule
Cells with $\mathrm{EDD} \geq 3$ & 9/9 & 8/9 & 7/9 & 5/9 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_auc_decay.pdf}
\caption{Binary pairwise AUC decay curves for three models (nucleus sampling, depths 0--5). Horizontal dashed lines mark $T \in \{0.65, 0.70, 0.75, 0.80\}$. All three curves converge below 0.70 at d4-vs-d5, while d3-vs-d4 AUC clusters in the range 0.750--0.784. Shaded bands: bootstrap 95\% CIs.}
\label{fig:auc_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Decision boundary dilution.}
\label{sec:atlas:edd:dilution}
EDD uses binary-pair classifiers to avoid \emph{decision boundary dilution}: in Pythia nucleus, d2\,vs.\,d3 AUC degrades from 0.804 (4-class) to 0.689 (6-class)---a 14.3\% relative decline despite identical data---because shared decision boundaries dilute per-pair capacity.
Binary classifiers eliminate this confound, isolating true adjacent-pair discriminability.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Strategy-Dependent Trajectories}
\label{sec:atlas:strategy}

The three decoding strategies induce qualitatively distinct degradation trajectories.
Nucleus ($p{=}0.95$) produces the most consistent accuracy (78.2--83.3\%) and serves as the reference trajectory.
Temperature ($\tau{=}0.9$) yields the highest accuracy in 2/3 models (GPT-2~XL 85.4\%, OLMo 82.0\%) because uniform logit scaling accelerates convergence to an attractor distribution~\cite{shumailov2024_nature}, amplifying the statistical fingerprint---but this acceleration creates a boundary reversal risk: temperature depth-$N$ features approximate nucleus/top-$k$ depth-$(N{+}1)$ features, causing cross-strategy classifiers to systematically overpredict depth (\S\ref{sec:generalization}).
Top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) produces the lowest accuracy (69.6--76.6\%) because hard vocabulary truncation removes depth-discriminative low-probability tokens, though feature direction is preserved (cross-model $\rho = 0.811$).
The strategy effect (8.4~pp range) exceeds the model effect (7.1~pp), indicating deployment-time knowledge of the decoding strategy matters at least as much as the generator model.

